$ git clone https://github.com/ester-project/ester.git

This will create a directory named ester that contains the source code of ESTER. Go to this directory, and create a build directory:

$ cd ester
mkdir build

Configure and check all dependencies are installed

Ensure you are in the build directory and run:

cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/local

Optional MESA EOS and opacity support
-------------------------------------

ESTER can optionally use the MESA equation of state (EOS) and opacity
(KAP) modules through LIBMESAC. This support is disabled by default and
is enabled at compile time with ESTER_USE_LIBMESAC=ON.

The current interface has been developed and tested with MESA r24.03.1
and the eos-interface branch of LIBMESAC:

MESA r24.03.1:
https://docs.mesastar.org/en/24.03.1/

LIBMESAC eos-interface:
https://github.com/mg477/libmesac/tree/eos-interface


1. Install MESA r24.03.1

Install the MESA SDK and MESA r24.03.1 following the MESA installation
instructions:

https://docs.mesastar.org/en/24.03.1/installation.html

Set MESA_DIR and MESASDK_ROOT according to the MESA installation
instructions.

MESA must be built with position-independent C objects because its static
libraries are linked into the ESTER shared library. Before compiling
MESA, add -fPIC to C_SPECIAL_FLAGS in

    $MESA_DIR/utils/makefile_header

for example:

    C_SPECIAL_FLAGS += -fPIC

Then compile MESA normally:

    cd $MESA_DIR
    ./install


2. Install LIBMESAC

Clone the eos-interface branch of LIBMESAC:

    git clone --branch eos-interface \
        https://github.com/mg477/libmesac.git

    cd libmesac
    ./mk

The LIBMESAC build script creates the build directory and, after a
successful build, runs the KAP and EOS tests. Set LIBMESAC_ROOT to this
build directory:

    export LIBMESAC_ROOT=/path/to/libmesac/build

The required headers and static libraries are then located in

    $LIBMESAC_ROOT/include
    $LIBMESAC_ROOT/lib


3. Build ESTER with MESA/LIBMESAC support

Once MESA and LIBMESAC have been installed and MESA_DIR,
MESASDK_ROOT and LIBMESAC_ROOT are defined, configure ESTER with:

    cd /path/to/ester/build

    cmake .. \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/local \
        -DESTER_USE_LIBMESAC=ON

    make
    make install

MESA/LIBMESAC support can also be combined with the compile-time NOPLOT
option:

    cmake .. \
        -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/local \
        -DESTER_USE_LIBMESAC=ON \
        -DNOPLOT=ON


4. Using the MESA microphysics

When ESTER has been compiled with ESTER_USE_LIBMESAC=ON, the MESA
equation of state and opacity can be selected independently at runtime:

    ester 1d -eos mesa
    ester 1d -opa mesa

or together:

    ester 1d -eos mesa -opa mesa

For example:

    ester 1d -noplot -M 5 -eos mesa -opa mesa -o M5_mesa.h5

If ESTER is compiled without ESTER_USE_LIBMESAC=ON, requesting
-eos mesa or -opa mesa will result in an error.

The standard ESTER build does not require MESA or LIBMESAC.
ESTER_USE_LIBMESAC is OFF by default.

Old installation:

ESTER is available under the GPL Version 3 license. It is hosted either on
on github (https://github.com/ester-project/ester).

To use the last version of the code, you can directly clone the last
development version form Git:

shell$ git clone https://github.com/ester-project/ester.git
shell$ cd ester
shell$ ./bootstrap

Installation of the ESTER binaries and library use the common GNU-based
process:
shell$ ./configure --prefix=...
shell$ make
shell$ make install

You can pass flags to the configure script to tune you installation:
- You can select the libs to use using LIBS:
  For instance if you want to use atlas libraries for blas, cblas and lapack
  libraries, you can use:

  shell$ ./configure LIBS="-L$HOME/local/lib -ltatlas"
  (provided that ATLAS is installed in $HOME/local/lib).

- You can select you Fortran compiler with FC and you c++ compiler with CXX.
- And you can use specific compiler flags with FCFLAGS and CXXFLAGS.

The default prefix is $HOME/local, thus adding $HOME/local/bin to your PATH
environment variable will allow you to run ESTER  without specifying the full
path to the binary.
